<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kosrae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kosrae is a volcanic island in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> of the western Pacific, and one of the four states of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>. Between about 1250 and 1400 its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> were united under a paramount ruler, the Tokosra, who governed from the stone city of Leluh on an islet off the east coast. Pohnpeian tradition names Kosrae as the homeland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isokelekel">Isokelekel</a>, who overthrew the Saudeleur dynasty.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kosrae.jpg" alt="Kosrae" width="320" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aotearoa · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kosrae.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kosrae, formerly known as Kusaie or Strong's Island, is the easternmost of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a>. It is a volcanic island and a state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>, with a land area of about 110 square kilometres. The main island, traditionally called Ualung, meaning the high island, rises to Mount Finkol at 634 metres. A fringing reef surrounds it, and the largest of the islets within the reef is Lelu Island. The state capital is Tofol.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence shows that the island was settled at least by the early years of the first millennium CE. The city of Leluh existed from about 1250, in its heyday had about 1,500 people and covered some 27 hectares, and had burial pyramids for the nobility. By the time of first contact with European travellers in 1824, Kosrae had a highly stratified society with matrilineal lineages and clans, in which nobles controlled land worked by commoners and settlements consisted of small groups of close relatives sharing a single cook house.</p><p>The Spanish navigator Álvaro de Saavedra made the first recorded Western sighting on 14 September 1529. The island was under nominal Spanish sovereignty from 1668 but was not effectively occupied until 1885, after the Carolines Question between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was settled by the arbitration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-leo-xiii">Pope Leo XIII</a>. The French corvette La Coquille, under Louis-Isidore Duperrey, visited in June 1824. Congregationalists set up the first mission post in 1852, and virtually the whole island had converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> by the 1870s. The captain and blackbirder Bully Hayes was shipwrecked there on 15 March 1874 and lived at Utwe for seven months, during which he terrorised the local people, before he was arrested and escaped.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> of 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> sold the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caroline-islands">Caroline Islands</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for 25 million pesetas, and the islands came under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, as the South Seas Mandate from 1919 to 1947. The Japanese fortified Kosrae in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, but no battles occurred there, and tunnel bunkers dug into the interior peaks survive. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> took over administration in 1945 as part of the Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands. Kosrae became a separate district in 1977 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a>, in which it is the only single-island state. Kosraean is its official language, and English has equal authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kosrae” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By tradition Isokelekel, son of the thunder god Nahn Sapwe, sailed from Katau in the east, a land often identified with <b>Kosrae</b>, with 333 followers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudeleur-dynasty">Saudeleur dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Between about 1250 and 1400 the chiefdoms of <b>Kosrae</b>, the easternmost of the Caroline Islands, were probably unified under a single paramount ruler, the Tokosra.</blockquote><a href="/state/kosrae-paramount-chiefdom">Kosrae paramount chiefdom</a></li></ul></section>
